schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Contributor Absence Factor (Bus Factor)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A metric defined as "the smallest number of contributors responsible
    for 50% of total contributions" — sort contributors by volume, add them
    up until crossing 50% of total contributions, and count how many it
    took.
  source_traditions:
    - >-
      CHAOSS project (Community Health Analytics in Open Source Software), a
      Linux Foundation effort — formerly called the "Bus Factor."
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Presented as a computable-by-hand (about ten minutes, from a commit log
    or wiki revision history) fragility check, meant to be run on a calendar
    rather than discovered during a burnout crisis.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - Bus Factor (the metric's older, more informal name)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      The number itself ("1 or 2") is a diagnostic trigger, not the point —
      the article stresses the real value is turning "who does half the
      work here?" into a scheduled question rather than a crisis discovery.
  tensions: []
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Renaming "Bus Factor" to "Contributor Absence Factor" is itself a
    deliberate reframing (removing a morbid metaphor) — using only one name
    risks losing the metric's more widely recognized prior identity for
    people searching under the older term.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/stewardship-roles.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
